In a middle school, there are 36 volunteers learning about laws in the first grade, 72 in the second grade, and 54 in the third grade. Stratified sampling is used to select a sample from these three grades, and it is known that there are 3 volunteers from the third grade in the sample.
(I) Calculate the number of volunteers from the first and second grades in the sample;
(II) Let $A_i$ (i=1, 2...) represent the volunteers from the first grade in the sample, and $a_i$ (i=1, 2...) represent the volunteers from the second grade in the sample. If 2 people are randomly selected from all the volunteers in the first and second grades of the sample, ① list all possible pairs of volunteers using notation for volunteers mentioned above, ② calculate the probability that both selected volunteers are from the same grade.

Among the following four propositions about the theorem of plane vector decomposition, the serial numbers of all correct propositions are. (Fill in the serial numbers of the propositions only)

① There is exactly one pair of non-parallel vectors in a plane that can serve as a basis for representing all vectors in that plane;  
② There are infinitely many pairs of non-parallel vectors in a plane that can serve as a basis for representing all vectors in that plane;  
③ The basis vectors of a plane vector can be perpendicular to each other;  
④ Any non-zero vector in a plane can be uniquely represented as a linear combination of three non-parallel vectors in that plane.

s:
According to the basic theorem of plane vectors:

① Any pair of non-parallel vectors in a plane can serve as a basis for representing all vectors in that plane; hence, this is incorrect;  
② There are infinitely many pairs of non-parallel vectors in a plane that can serve as a basis for representing all vectors in that plane; hence, this is correct;  
③ The basis vectors of a plane vector, as long as they are not collinear, can also be perpendicular to each other; hence, this is correct;  
④ Any non-zero vector in a plane can be uniquely represented as a linear combination of two non-parallel vectors in that plane. If there are three non-collinear vectors, the representation is not unique; hence, this is incorrect.  
Therefore, the answer is: $\boxed{2, 3}$.

