There are 20 products among which 5 are defective, and the rest are up to standard. Two products are drawn one after the other without replacement. Calculate the probability of:
(1) Drawing a defective product on the first draw;
(2) Drawing a defective product on both the first and the second draws;
(3) Drawing a defective product on the second draw, given that the first product drawn was defective.

Which of the following random events has a random variable $X$ that follows a hypergeometric distribution? Fill in the sequence number.  
① Tossing a coin 3 times, the number of times it lands heads up is denoted as $X$;  
② Selecting 5 outstanding student leaders from a group of 10, which includes 7 males and 3 females, and denoting the number of females as $X$;  
③ A shooter has a hit rate of 0.8, and now shoots at a target once, denoting the number of hits as $X$;  
④ In a box, there are 4 white balls and 3 black balls. Each time one ball is drawn without replacement, and $X$ is the number of times a black ball is drawn first.

Among the following random variable $X$'s distribution, which does not belong to a binomial distribution is __________.  
1. According to a report by CCTV News, the probability of a computer being infected by a certain virus after downloading data from a certain website within the next week is 0.65. Suppose that the number of times a computer is infected by this virus after downloading data from the website $n$ times within this week is $X$;  
2. The probability of a shooter hitting the target is $p$. Suppose each shot is independent, and the number of shots needed to hit the target for the first time is $X$;  
3. The probability of a shooter hitting the target is $p$. Suppose each shot is independent, and the number of times the target is hit after $n$ shots is $X$;  
4. Near a certain bus station, there is a gas station. The probability of a car going to this gas station for refueling after leaving the station is 0.6. On National Day, 50 cars leave the station. Assuming that the cars going to this gas station for refueling within a day are independent, the number of cars going to the gas station for refueling is $X$.

A box contains 12 balls that are identical except for their colors. A ball is randomly drawn from the box. The probability of drawing a red ball is $\dfrac{5}{12}$, the probability of drawing a black ball is $\dfrac{1}{3}$, the probability of drawing a white ball is $\dfrac{1}{6}$, and the probability of drawing a green ball is $\dfrac{1}{12}$. Find:  
(1) The probability of drawing a ball that is either red or black;  
(2) The probability of drawing a ball that is either red, black, or white.

A class selects 3 people from 6 class officers (including 4 male and 2 female officers) to participate in the school's mandatory labor.

(I) Calculate the probability that male officer A or female officer B is selected;

(II) Define "male officer A is selected" as event A, and "female officer B is selected" as event B. Calculate $P(B)$ and $P(B|A)$.

The relevant departments want to understand the popularization of knowledge about the prevention of H1N1 influenza in schools, so they designed a questionnaire with 10 questions and conducted a survey in various schools. Two classes, A and B, from a certain middle school were randomly selected, with 5 students from each class participating in the survey. The scores of the 5 students in class A were: 5, 8, 9, 9, 9; the scores of the 5 students in class B were: 6, 7, 8, 9, 10.  
(Ⅰ) Please estimate which class, A or B, has more stable questionnaire scores;  
(Ⅱ) If we consider the scores of the 5 students in class B as a population and use a simple random sampling method to draw a sample with a size of 2, calculate the probability that the absolute difference between the sample mean and the population mean is not less than 1.

A conference room is illuminated by 3 lamps, each using one energy-saving bulb of the same model. Assuming whether each lamp can illuminate normally only depends on the lifespan of the bulb, the probability that the lifespan of this model of bulb exceeds 1 year is 0.8, and the probability that the lifespan exceeds 2 years is 0.3. Bulb replacement is carried out annually, only replacing those that have failed, without replacing any that are still working.  
(I) During the first bulb replacement, calculate the probability of not needing to replace any bulbs and the probability of needing to replace 2 bulbs;  
(II) During the second bulb replacement, for one of the lamps, calculate the probability that the bulb needs to be replaced.

